From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: trace the FWDL ring and dump the ROM reply to the patch commands

The patch download on AN7581 dies 15 ms after PATCH_FINISH_REQ and the
endpoint leaves the bus, so nothing after the failure can be read back:

  diag[pre]  lpctl(0xe00f0)=0x00000001
  diag[post] lpctl(0xe00f0)=0x00000001   SRAM unchanged vs. pre
  diag[start] issuing PATCH_FINISH_REQ
  pcieport: AER: Correctable, Physical Layer (RxErr)
  pcieport: AER: Uncorrectable (Fatal), Data Link Layer (SDES)

Two things are still unknown and they lead to completely different fixes:

  1. did the 12 KB scatter actually leave the host?  MCU_CMD_FW_SCATTER
     goes through Q_TX_FWDL and is queued with wait_resp = false, so a
     ring that never moves looks exactly like a delivery that worked.
  2. did the ROM accept PATCH_START_REQ?  mcu_parse_response() only
     matches the sequence number, so a failure status is indistinguish-
     able from success.

For (1), log every FWDL descriptor push together with the hardware write
index and the DDONE bit, and log the tail advance in
pci_dma_tx_tail_sync().  DDONE can only be set by the device, so four
"fwdl done" lines prove the payload really reached the chip's DMA
engine, while a stuck tail proves it never left the ring.

For (2), dump the raw reply of PATCH_START_REQ (0x05) and
PATCH_FINISH_REQ (0x07), which are otherwise silently discarded.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
@@ -1151,6 +1151,18 @@ pci_dma_tx_tail_sync(struct pci_trans *trans,
 
 		q->tail = (q->tail+1) % q->q_size;
 		done++;
+
+		/*
+		 * Bring-up diagnostic: this only runs once the device has set
+		 * DDONE on a descriptor, so it proves the FWDL ring really did
+		 * hand the payload over to the chip's DMA engine.
+		 */
+		if (q->q_attr == Q_TX_FWDL)
+			dev_err(to_device(trans),
+				"diag fwdl done: tail=%u head=%u didx=%u\n",
+				q->tail, q->head,
+				bus_read(to_bus_trans(trans),
+					 q->hw_didx_addr));
 	}
 
 	return done;
@@ -2476,6 +2488,24 @@ pci_dma_tx_queue_skb_raw(struct pci_trans *trans, u8 qid,
 	spin_unlock(&q->lock);
 
 	pci_dma_kick_queue(trans, q);
+
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up diagnostic: the ROM patch payload is the only thing that
+	 * goes through Q_TX_FWDL and it is fire-and-forget, so a ring that
+	 * never moves looks exactly like a download that worked.  Report the
+	 * descriptor we just pushed and the hardware write index right after
+	 * the kick, plus the DDONE bit that only the device can set.
+	 */
+	if (q->q_attr == Q_TX_FWDL) {
+		struct pci_dma_desc *d =
+			q->cb[(q->head + q->q_size - 1) % q->q_size].alloc_va;
+
+		dev_err(to_device(trans),
+			"diag fwdl push: len=%d head=%u tail=%u didx=%u dw1=0x%08x\n",
+			skb->len, q->head, q->tail,
+			bus_read(to_bus_trans(trans), q->hw_didx_addr),
+			le32_to_cpu(d->dw1));
+	}
 	return 0;
 err:
 	return -ENOMEM;
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu.c
@@ -98,6 +98,30 @@ mcu_parse_response(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev,
 		mcu_txblk->cmd != result->cid)
 		return -EAGAIN;
 
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up diagnostic: mcu_parse_response() only matches on seq, so a
+	 * ROM that answers PATCH_START_REQ with a failure status looks exactly
+	 * like a successful one.  Dump the raw payload for the two patch
+	 * commands so the ROM gets a chance to say what it did not like.
+	 */
+	if (mcu_txblk->cmd == 0x05 || mcu_txblk->cmd == 0x07) {
+		const u8 *p = (const u8 *)rx_info.rx_data;
+		char dbuf[3 * 8 + 1];
+		int i, n = rx_info.rx_len;
+
+		if (n > 8)
+			n = 8;
+		if (n < 0)
+			n = 0;
+		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+			snprintf(dbuf + i * 3, 4, "%02x ", p[i]);
+		dbuf[n * 3] = '\0';
+
+		dev_err(dev->dev,
+			"diag mcu resp cmd=0x%02x seq=%d len=%d:%s\n",
+			mcu_txblk->cmd, rx_info.seq, rx_info.rx_len, dbuf);
+	}
+
 	if (mcu_write_back_resp(&rx_info, mcu_txblk->resp))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
